= Getting Started

The file `python_course.zip` on Moodle contains all the files you need for the
course.

Everything is also available on the #link("https://github.com/oscarbranson/esc_computing")[course GitHub repository]

All the course files are #link("https://jupyter.org/")[Jupyter Notebooks],
identified by the `.ipynb` extension.

= Working with Notebooks

You'll need to have a way to open these on your own computer. There are
two main ways to do this:

+ Use an online platform, like
  #link("https://colab.research.google.com/")[Google Colab] (no
  installation required). You have access to this via your University
  Google account. You just need to extract the contents of
  `python_course.zip` into a folder in your Google Drive, then the
  `.ipynb` files should just open in Colab.

+ Install python on your computer (installation required. If you plan
  to do things with Python in future (e.g. in your project), this is worth
  spending some time setting up now. There are few ways to do this. One of
  the most straightforward is to use
  #link("https://www.anaconda.com/")[Anaconda], which provides an 'out of
  the box' python environment with lots of common scientific packages.
  This is a good place to start, and you can see detailed installation
  instructions #link("https://docs.anaconda.com/anaconda/install/")[here].
  This will allow you to run the notebooks on your own computer. First,
  extract `python_course.zip` into a folder on your computer. Then open
  the 'Anaconda Navigator' program and launch JupyterLab. Once in Jupyter,
  navigate to python course folder the on your computer, and you will be
  able to access the notebooks.

There are many other ways to interact with Python notebooks. For
example, #link("https://code.visualstudio.com/")[VSCode] and
#link("https://www.jetbrains.com/pycharm/")[PyCharm] are two common
ones. Ask in the sessions if you'd like to find out more about using
these.
